# Curator repository-sealing fixture scope

All six native grader invocations used **psf__requests-6028**, with its qualified
187 designated checks. This fixture did not execute the Requests 5414 to 6028
model trajectory or its exogenous upstream update.

The first source state was the curator-held reference candidate for 6028. The
second retained that same reference candidate and added a neutral public comment
to an existing production file. Both states contained 97 source files. A third
event read the second accepted state from native storage and checked exact source
restoration. Reference source and patch contents remain in curator custody.

The six actual grader invocations comprise two initial candidate scores and four
fresh replays: first publication, interrupted second publication, recovery, and
the third event's accepted-source read. All six completed all 187 designated
checks successfully. Their container times total 31.625 seconds; native context,
memory, publication, and caller overhead are additional recorded work. No
provider was called.

This establishes the qualified service's ability to publish a full checkpoint,
construct a delta, survive an actual process exit after seal persistence, finish
the pending compare-and-swap, and consume the accepted source in a fresh process.
It also establishes four bounded admission rejections. All four proof leaves were
replaced on transition. Receipts are unsigned, and native verification uses
integrity mode. The result provides no model efficacy, avoided verification work,
token saving, theorem-proof, or adversarial oracle-integrity claim.

The actual two-issue controller comparison is a separate evaluation requiring its
own frozen profile, model receipts, source transition, scoring, and complete cost
ledger. The signed-off code identities for this service fixture are recorded in
`qualification_admission_v1.json`; this addendum does not alter that frozen
admission artifact or its qualification results.
